======================== NOTE: if any permission issue come ,, follow this 

Step 1: Clean up the root-owned folders

Why it was needed: The job failed with rm: cannot remove ... Permission denied. The runner runs jobs as the gitlab-runner user, 
but the build folders were owned by root, so it couldn’t delete them.

Command 1: see who owns the files

ls -la /home/gitlab-runner/builds/CG9ZXi_rh/0/sachin-devops-lab/

. ls lists files, -l shows details (owner, permissions), -a includes hidden files.
. Your output showed root root as owner. That was the proof of the problem.

Command 2: delete the old build folders

rm -rf /home/gitlab-runner/builds/*

Command 3: give ownership back to the runner user

chown -R gitlab-runner:gitlab-runner /home/gitlab-runner

Command 4: verify

ls -la /home/gitlab-runner/builds/

Step 2: Make sure only the real service is running

Why it was needed: You had several extra gitlab-runner run processes, started by hand as root from the terminal.
When they ran jobs, they created root-owned folders. Only the systemd service should run the runner.

Command 1: list the runner processes

bash
ps aux | grep gitlab-runner
ps aux lists all running processes, with the user, PID and start time.
grep gitlab-runner filters the list to lines containing that word.
The first column showed root for the extra processes, and pts/1 meant they came from your terminal.

Command 2: kill the extra processes

bash
kill -9 7155 7202 8248 10906
kill sends a signal to a process, and the numbers are the PIDs (process IDs) to stop.
-9 is SIGKILL, which stops a process immediately and can’t be ignored. It was needed because those processes were suspended (state T).
PID 7112 was not in this list, because it is the real service.

Command 3: verify only the service remains

bash
ps aux | grep "gitlab-runner run" | grep -v grep
grep -v grep removes the grep command itself from the results.
You should see one line only, PID 7112 with --user gitlab-runner. That is what your screenshot showed.

Command 4: check the service status

bash
systemctl status gitlab-runner --no-pager
systemctl status shows whether a systemd service is running.
--no-pager prints everything at once instead of opening a scrolling view that can freeze your terminal.
Your output showed active (running), which is correct.

Step 3: Confirm ownership once more

On the EC2 terminal: chown -R gitlab-runner:gitlab-runner /home/gitlab-runner

Step 4: Check the shell profile files
These can cause “prepare environment: exit status 1” on shell runners:

mv /home/gitlab-runner/.bash_logout /home/gitlab-runner/.bash_logout.bak

Note: If it says “No such file”, that’s fine, skip it.

Step 5: Watch the runner logs

Keep this open in the EC2 terminal: journalctl -u gitlab-runner -f --no-pager

